      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my circuits final and im a bit stuck. can u explain how a capacitor acts differently in a dc circuit vs an ac circuit? also what's the formula for capacitive reactance? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In DC circuits, a capacitor stores energy in an electric field, and does so without energy oscillating. In contrast, AC circuits cause a capacitor to block or react to the changing voltage, resulting in a phase shift and an inductive reactance-like effect. \n\nThe formula for capacitive reactance, which is the opposition to the flow of current through a capacitor in an AC circuit, is Xc = 1 / (2 * π * f * C), where Xc is the capacitive reactance, f is the frequency of the AC voltage, and C is the capacitance.",
